// @run
// @log 7
// @hint redundant type annotation: `c0` is inferred as `{r: i32}` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `g` is inferred as `(Dot) => Dot` — remove it to lean on inference
// D351 — THE SAME `$fnsig` SEAM D199 CLOSED, ONE TABLE OVER, AND THE CLOSE IS IN THE POOL.
//
// `arm-layout-twin-fnsig-decline.vl` beside this file is two ARMS of one layout keying
// `V0;` and `V1;` over one `uVarHeap` row; this is one ARM and one DECLARED STRUCT keying
// `V0;` and `s0;` over ONE heap type, because `mAssignTypeIndices`' `uVarSTwinAt` arm gives
// the arm the struct's `sHeapIdx` row (D280's cross-table merge). Two structurally
// identical functypes at two indices of one rec group are DISTINCT types in WasmGC, so the
// `call_indirect` operand and the callee's declared functype disagreed: `vl check` rc 0, the
// module LOADED, and it trapped `indirect call type mismatch` inside `viaC` with nothing
// printed.
//
// THE HINTS ARE DECLARED, NOT DESIGNED AWAY, for the reason the sibling states: `c0: Circle`
// and `g: (Dot) => Dot` are what force the ARM spelling and the STRUCT spelling to meet at
// one call, and the checker calling both redundant is the same acceptance D280 quotes — the
// two names ARE one type to the checker while they were two `$fnsig`s to the emitter.
//
// WHAT CLOSED IT is not a fifth producer and not a sixth canonicalisation: it is the POOL.
// `cloSigTwin` folds two pool entries whose functypes render BYTE-IDENTICALLY onto one type
// index, and `cloSigTypeIdxAt` is the one place a pool position becomes an index, so the
// callee's declared functype and the call site's operand move together by construction. The
// KEYS are untouched — `sigParamKindAt` still reads `V0;` here, which is exactly what the
// REFUSED key-level fold could not preserve (it cost six `runs` cells; this costs none).
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type Dot = { r: i32 }
const c0: Circle = { r: 7 }
function viaC(f: (Circle) => Circle, x: Circle) { print(f(x).r) }
const g: (Dot) => Dot = (q: Dot) => q
viaC(g, c0)
